1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,857
Let X be some arbitrary set and suppose towards a contradiction that there is

2
00:00:04,957 --> 00:00:08,076
a 1-1 and onto map f from X to the power set of X.

3
00:00:08,133 --> 00:00:11,130
Where onto just means that every element in the power set

4
00:00:11,230 --> 00:00:13,466
of X gets mapped to by some element from X.

5
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:17,035
We can define the diagonal set D as the subset of X consisting

6
00:00:17,135 --> 00:00:20,695
of the elements which are not contained in their image under f.

7
00:00:20,733 --> 00:00:22,413
This is a subset of X, and therefore

8
00:00:22,513 --> 00:00:24,786
by definition exists somewhere in the power set.

9
00:00:24,866 --> 00:00:29,352
By our assumption that the map is onto, there should

10
00:00:29,452 --> 00:00:32,969
be some element a in X, such that f(a)=D.

11
00:00:33,066 --> 00:00:38,452
By definition of D, we know that a in D iff a not in f(a).

12
00:00:38,533 --> 00:00:41,748
But D=f(a), so we get that a in f(a)

13
00:00:41,848 --> 00:00:45,799
iff a not in f(a), which is a contradiction.

14
00:00:45,866 --> 00:00:49,473
So, it must be that our map was not onto, and therefore the size

15
00:00:49,573 --> 00:00:52,427
of the power set of D is larger than the size of D.

